WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.509
It's great to welcome Brian GREENE to the
program today, who's a professor of physics

00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:10.970
and mathematics and director of Columbia University's
Center for Theoretical Physics, also known

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:14.730
for his groundbreaking discoveries in superstring
theory.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:19.260
And author of many books including I believe
most recently it's until the end of time,

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:20.260
right?

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:21.260
That's your latest.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:22.260
Yes, that's correct.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:23.260
Exactly.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:28.920
So maybe to get into our conversation when
somebody maybe is trying to understand not

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:34.970
something like superstring theory or the nuts
and bolts, but more generally, what types

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:38.230
of questions does your work seek to answer
to?

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:40.950
To have a broader understanding of what you're
doing?

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.280
How would you explain that to them?

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.670
Well, certainly in my news book that you just
made reference to until the end of time, the

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:55.280
goal is to give the curious person a sense
of how they fit within the cosmic hole.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.809
I mean, the journey that I take in that book
is from the very beginning of time, walking

00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:05.830
through the sequence of steps that, you know,
life and conscious beings and on to the very

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:06.830
far future.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:13.750
And within that cosmic unfolding, my goal
is to give the person a sense of where they

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:19.270
came from, the environment that they're in
now, where it came from and where things are

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:25.510
going with ultimately a new perspective on
why things matter.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:30.830
Does the day to day of sort of like imagine
thinking of superstring theory, for example,

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:34.460
when you're working on something like superstring
theory?

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:39.320
Does it feel like it's connected directly
to answering those questions or does it feel

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:44.470
like it starts to get removed and sort of
operate in a more theoretical space?

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:50.720
Well, we like to use the big questions as
motivation.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:57.360
We deeply want to know the full story of creation,
the full story of how the universe began.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.520
We'd love to understand the deep nature of
space and time.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.680
These kinds of questions are why many of us
certainly.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.450
For me, it's the case went into the field
in the first place.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:12.439
But your question is a good one, because when
you're in the midst of doing the research,

00:02:12.439 --> 00:02:15.019
it's like any other subject.

00:02:15.019 --> 00:02:23.299
The detail, the daily grind, if you will,
can often be fairly disconnected from the

00:02:23.299 --> 00:02:25.099
big overarching goals.

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:31.159
So, you know, I have students that come to
me and they want to work in physics and all

00:02:31.159 --> 00:02:34.269
they have in mind are the big, huge questions.

00:02:34.269 --> 00:02:40.199
And when I describe them, I hate to make progress
toward those questions.

00:02:40.199 --> 00:02:41.895
You've got to do mathematical calculations,
you gotta do experiments, you've got to write

00:02:41.895 --> 00:02:44.639
computer programs, you've got to do simulations.

00:02:44.639 --> 00:02:47.140
For many of them, that's a bit of a shock.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:51.110
But that's the way you make progress in the
day to day sense.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:55.930
How would you sort of ballpark the progress
that has been made like over the last 10,

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:59.359
50 and 200 years on answering some of these
questions?

00:02:59.359 --> 00:03:03.999
In other words, there's camps that say we're
never really going to know the origins because

00:03:03.999 --> 00:03:09.249
we can't can't really go back in a way that
would give us those answers.

00:03:09.249 --> 00:03:13.269
There's others who say maybe we will, but
like aside from the end point that we may

00:03:13.269 --> 00:03:14.620
or may not get, too.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:16.519
What's the what's been the progress that has
been made?

00:03:16.519 --> 00:03:19.159
Well, it's been utterly stunning, right?

00:03:19.159 --> 00:03:25.430
If you if you go to the furthest extent of
the timeline that you mentioned, go back 200

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:29.269
years to say the early eighteen hundreds.

00:03:29.269 --> 00:03:33.989
We were just beginning to gain an understanding
of some of the most rudimentary forces at

00:03:33.989 --> 00:03:38.040
work in the universe, how electricity and
magnetism worked.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:43.609
The early experiments are just taking place,
experiments on the nature of light.

00:03:43.609 --> 00:03:45.430
These were vital developments.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:51.109
But when you compare them with what we understand
today, the insights that have come from special

00:03:51.109 --> 00:03:57.269
relativity, Einstein generativity again, Einstein
quantum mechanics and on through the developments

00:03:57.269 --> 00:04:02.579
in unification that have swept through the
20th century and into the 21st.

00:04:02.579 --> 00:04:08.329
I mean, right now we can do mathematical calculations
that tell us what the universe is like a fraction

00:04:08.329 --> 00:04:10.799
of a second after the beginning.

00:04:10.799 --> 00:04:15.480
And then we can use those mathematical insights
to make predictions for what those conditions

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.859
way back then should yield.

00:04:17.859 --> 00:04:20.620
For our observations today.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:26.440
And many of those predictions are borne out
to fantastic accuracy by powerful equipment

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:31.939
that we launch into space that takes the temperature
of the night sky that looks deep into the

00:04:31.939 --> 00:04:34.110
heart of black holes.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:39.530
So it is stunning how far we've come in the
last 200 years.

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:45.849
What do you think has been the effect of that
on sort of the human psyche as we exist in

00:04:45.849 --> 00:04:46.849
the world?

00:04:46.849 --> 00:04:47.849
And the reason I ask.

00:04:47.849 --> 00:04:54.620
I mean, is it we also it's hard to separate
that from technological advance medical science.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:56.139
Changing so drastically.

00:04:56.139 --> 00:04:57.379
Travel like it.

00:04:57.379 --> 00:04:59.990
It's difficult because those things have also
changed so much.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:04.960
But what's your sense of how our understanding
and day to day lives are different based on

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.099
what we now know that we didn't 200 years
ago?

00:05:08.099 --> 00:05:15.440
Well, I don't think you can give a uniform
blanket answer for all human minds.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:21.699
There are some of us who are deeply interested
in these ideas, even if we're not doing research

00:05:21.699 --> 00:05:28.219
on the topic and have allowed the insights
of modern science to affect our outlook on

00:05:28.219 --> 00:05:36.280
the world and given us the laudable perspective
that there are true qualities of reality that

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:41.699
research both experimental and theoretical
equipment, together with, say, mathematical

00:05:41.699 --> 00:05:44.759
insights, can reveal.

00:05:44.759 --> 00:05:49.280
There are others, though, who enjoy the fruits
of those undertakings.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.401
As you mentioned, the technology that has
emerged from the deeper understanding, but

00:05:54.401 --> 00:05:58.120
have not taken in that mindset.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:03.189
And it's really unfortunate, especially in
the situations that we find ourselves in today,

00:06:03.189 --> 00:06:10.539
when there are some who feel that reality
ultimately is a matter of opinion.

00:06:10.539 --> 00:06:12.319
It's not a matter of opinion.

00:06:12.319 --> 00:06:17.020
I'm not trying to downgrade the value of opinions
on certain issues.

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:23.430
But in terms of true qualities of reality
here on planet Earth and in the wider cosmos,

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:26.530
there is such a thing as right and wrong.

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:29.069
There is such a thing as fact and fiction.

00:06:29.069 --> 00:06:37.909
There is such a thing as expertise that can
yield insights that can determine the correct

00:06:37.909 --> 00:06:40.569
course of action on vital issues.

00:06:40.569 --> 00:06:46.900
And not everyone has taken in this basic quality
of scientific advancement over the past two,

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:47.900
three hundred years.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:48.900
Right.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:54.039
Well, so I mean, now we have the phrase alternative
facts is an interesting one that that has

00:06:54.039 --> 00:06:55.039
surfaced.

00:06:55.039 --> 00:06:59.610
And these ideas of, you know, if I don't like
your conclusion, I can just kind of reject

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:02.680
the premises that you use to build that up.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:06.870
Do you think that that's been more acutely
weaponized recently in society?

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:09.379
It certainly has.

00:07:09.379 --> 00:07:16.719
Especially when you've got leaders who have
that very perspective, or at least leaders

00:07:16.719 --> 00:07:25.520
who leverage that perspective by using it
as a tool or as you describe as a weapon to

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:29.050
advance certain political perspectives.

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:32.759
And that is deeply troubling.

00:07:32.759 --> 00:07:39.120
And as we're beginning to see, has the capacity
to yield deep tragedy.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.370
What's the solution in your mind to that?

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:45.139
Because I've read some interesting pieces
that say it's not as simple as we have to

00:07:45.139 --> 00:07:50.909
start teaching critical thinking and philosophy
and epistemology earlier that there's a bigger

00:07:50.909 --> 00:07:54.490
divide and a bigger picture issue than than
simply that.

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:58.520
I agree, although I would say that that is
part of the solution.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:06.110
I. I can't help the optimistic side of my
personality from saying I just feel that if

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:12.340
we could get kids at an early stage to really
recognize the power of scientific insight,

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:13.360
not abstractly.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:20.050
But, you know, I've taught kids and I've seen
them undergo that transition from confusion

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:26.650
about something to mastery, even if it's a
small element of math or physics.

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:33.800
And that move from confusion to clarity can
be life changing because your way of thinking

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:39.919
about the world and your recognition of your
power to understand the world can radically

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:40.919
change.

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:42.029
But I agree, that's not the full.

00:08:42.029 --> 00:08:48.330
It's not the full solution, because we find
ourselves in an era when many people and by

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:51.250
many I don't exactly know the number that
I'm talking about.

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:54.120
So don't hold me to sort of details of numbers
here.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:59.930
But there is a segment of the population that
makes decisions not based on thinking through

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:05.990
policy, but rather who else is supporting
that policy.

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:12.600
It's the group think it's whether or not you
want to be on that group or the other group

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:18.410
as opposed to a rational evaluation of policy
and perspective.

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:27.329
And that kind of identity politics needs to
needs to shift because that is not the appropriate,

00:09:27.329 --> 00:09:30.220
useful way to set policy.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:33.570
Yeah, I'm curious what you would think about
the motivations of those.

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.110
So let's just to give an example.

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:37.130
The Tea Party in 2010.

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:38.130
Right.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:43.410
This was a movement that succeeded to the
extent that it did because of individuals

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:48.709
voting for people who were pushing policies
that were not going to be good for them as

00:09:48.709 --> 00:09:49.709
individuals.

00:09:49.709 --> 00:09:54.910
But like Jonathan hydt, for example, argues
they were policies that.

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:58.910
Identified as good for the group in which
they were members or a membership maybe is

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:04.899
a bit of a too formal a term for it, isn't
that kind of individual versus group thinking

00:10:04.899 --> 00:10:07.290
a factor in what you're describing here?

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:08.290
It's huge.

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:10.540
It's exactly what I'm describing.

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:17.890
You know, if you were to and Jonathan probably
did because he is a great social scientist

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:19.389
and goes out and gets the data.

00:10:19.389 --> 00:10:21.959
I imagine that they've collected data.

00:10:21.959 --> 00:10:28.080
If you interview the very people who are supporting
a given policy and ask them why they are supporting

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:29.080
it.

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:33.209
Some will have thought it through, no doubt,
but many will not have thought it through

00:10:33.209 --> 00:10:37.130
because that's not the way that the decision
is made.

00:10:37.130 --> 00:10:41.699
Rather, the decision is made in the manner
that you describe.

00:10:41.699 --> 00:10:48.380
Do you want to be identified as being part
of that group of people who, for whatever

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:53.389
reason, you feel more of a kinship with?

00:10:53.389 --> 00:10:59.209
Or do you want to break ranks with the group
that you feel that kinship with in order to

00:10:59.209 --> 00:11:06.430
follow a policy that is not widely accepted
by the initial group?

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:14.850
And unfortunately, that kind of perspective
and that kind of decision making is prevalent

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:20.020
and it's not the kind of decision making that
ultimately is good for the world.

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:25.279
So to step back from zoom out from this a
tiny bit, I have this idea that maybe is very

00:11:25.279 --> 00:11:31.160
naive and I'm curious what you think about
it, which is if we were to make contact with

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:36.380
alien intelligence and find out it's real,
if we were to figure out some of the big answers

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:41.959
that people in your field are looking for
about our our place in the universe, etc.,

00:11:41.959 --> 00:11:49.110
I have this idea that issues like gay marriage
and debt and deficit and stuff like this would

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:54.139
evaporate because everybody would immediately
have this awakening as a result of this new

00:11:54.139 --> 00:11:55.550
information that we have.

00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:59.560
Is that incredibly naive, which would we just
kind of adjust and keep having the same disagreements

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:00.560
we're having?

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:05.190
Well, I think the answer depends a lot on
the details.

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:06.540
Right.

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:14.730
It's certainly the case that having a common
enemy is a powerful force of unification.

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:20.770
Fortunately, and unfortunately, at the same
time, when we can identify a common enemy,

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:27.510
it allows us to bind together into an even
larger group if that will aid in our survival.

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:33.470
And look, many of our predilections emerged
from evolutionary pressures across, you know,

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:36.199
tens of thousands of generations.

00:12:36.199 --> 00:12:43.399
And the ability to come together into socialized
groups has been an important means by which

00:12:43.399 --> 00:12:47.010
we have gotten to our place of dominance in
the world.

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:53.210
So it's not surprising that we have the sort
of inbuilt reaction to certain kinds of pressures,

00:12:53.210 --> 00:12:57.060
certain kinds of challenges, and once a challenge
is a common enemy.

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:01.779
But, you know, I like to think that if we
encountered the aliens, maybe it won't be

00:13:01.779 --> 00:13:06.870
an enemy, maybe be a very friendly relationship.

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:12.600
And if that were the case, maybe they would
reveal deep truths that somehow the world

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:14.580
would listen to.

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:21.060
But I have a feeling that it's and maybe over
a long stretch of time that might be possible.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:25.819
But the notion that I think you are articulating
that all of a sudden we sort of lift ourselves

00:13:25.819 --> 00:13:29.699
up from thinking about all of the issues here.

00:13:29.699 --> 00:13:35.089
And all of this now is sort of secondary to
this larger kind of conversation we're having

00:13:35.089 --> 00:13:36.380
having with aliens.

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:40.720
I suspect that would not happen, at least
not on the kinds of instantaneous timescales

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:41.720
that you have in mind.

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:46.110
Well, part of what makes me doubt it would
happen at all is that humans seem to have

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:50.899
this incredible ability to just adjust to
what reality becomes and it becomes the new

00:13:50.899 --> 00:13:55.890
baseline and things sort of return to some
kind of baseline in some way.

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:57.649
Yeah, I no, it's certainly the case.

00:13:57.649 --> 00:14:03.260
You know, I was on an airplane at JFK on the
tarmac on September 11th, 2001.

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:10.040
And I could I saw the the plane hit the tower
from my seat in the plane.

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:14.800
I had a clear shot right from Queens into
Manhattan.

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:20.351
And so there was a sense at that moment that
that the world would completely change, that

00:14:20.351 --> 00:14:25.060
nobody would live their lives the way that
they once did.

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:27.420
And that was true for a while.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:32.629
And there certainly have been changes that
occurred by virtue of 9/11, without a doubt.

00:14:32.629 --> 00:14:38.360
But I think many people reverted at least
pretty close to the lives that they led if

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:42.100
they weren't directly part of the tragedy
or if they didn't have a loved one who is

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:44.160
directly part of the tragedy.

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:45.160
We do have this.

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:51.149
You can call it resilience, this ability to
sort of revert back to the way that we once

00:14:51.149 --> 00:14:52.149
were.

00:14:52.149 --> 00:14:53.870
And so I kind of agree.

00:14:53.870 --> 00:15:01.589
With you that that is the default development
that we certainly have seen happen over and

00:15:01.589 --> 00:15:08.319
over again, that we find a way of sort of
acclimating to the new environment and taking

00:15:08.319 --> 00:15:15.820
our old lives and fitting it in to the new
jigsaw puzzle as best as we can and then going

00:15:15.820 --> 00:16:46.889
forward from there.

00:16:46.889 --> 00:17:59.930
Let's pause our conversation with professor

00:17:59.930 --> 00:18:27.290
of physics and Mathematics.

00:18:27.290 --> 00:19:35.880
Brian

00:19:35.880 --> 00:20:08.570
GREENE there.

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:19.050
The

00:20:19.050 --> 00:21:52.170
full conversation will be on The David Pakman
Show YouTube channel at YouTube.com.

00:21:52.170 --> 00:22:00.420
Splash The David Pakman Show.

